  "text": "**Solution 2** has the correct answer, $q^* = \\frac{N+\\alpha+4}{N}$. I could not check it against the source paper, so this rests on the internal analysis.\n\n- **Solutions 1 and 3** both give $\\frac{N+\\alpha+2}{N}$, from the $L^2$-critical scaling of the pure nonlocal term $|u|^q$. That scaling argument ignores that $F$ contains the lower-critical term $|u|^{(N+\\alpha)/N}$, whose cross term with $G(u)\\sim u^q$ controls the threshold. Solution 3's admissibility check also fails: $\\frac{N+\\alpha+2}{N} < \\frac{N+\\alpha}{N-2}$ holds only when $\\alpha < 2$, not \"since $\\alpha > -2$.\"\n- **Solution 2** locates the threshold where the gradient term ($L^{-2}$) and the cross term ($L^{-\\gamma}$, with $\\gamma = \\frac{N}{2}(q-\\bar p)$) balance, giving $\\gamma = 2$, i.e. $q = \\frac{N+\\alpha}{N} + \\frac{4}{N}$. Its admissibility check is the strongest evidence: $\\frac{N+\\alpha+4}{N} < \\frac{N+\\alpha}{N-2}$ holds exactly when $\\alpha > N-4$, which is precisely the standing hypothesis $\\alpha > \\max\\{0, N-4\\}$ in the problem. That match is unlikely to be coincidental.\n\nCHOICE: 2",
